{"schema":"plectis_maths_universe_excerpts_v2","paper":"erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface","excerpts":[["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b10a","A finite-grid condition gives a\nnonintegral pair","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math></span> be finite and\nnonempty. Suppose that <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup></math></span>\nfor every <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></math></span> and that </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> for some\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b10b","A sufficient quantified finite-grid\ncondition","<p><em> Suppose that for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span> there are <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span>, a depth <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi></math></span>, and a finite nonempty <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math></span> such that,\nwith <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></math></span>, all the hypotheses of\nTheorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:b10a\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b10a\">6.56</a>\nhold. Then <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>.\nIndeed, rationality would make all tail differences on every\nsufficiently large LCM grid integral, whereas the finite-grid theorem\nsupplies a nonintegral pair on such a grid. The example above verifies\none grid; it does not establish the quantified hypothesis.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b12","A finite carry test implies\nnonintegrality","<p><em> For each integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>z</mi></math></span> with <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, define </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nSuppose that for every such <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>z</mi></math></span> there\nis an <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi></math></span> with </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≥</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>. Indeed, an\nintegral value of the tail difference would be one of the <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> initial candidates. Its\nrecurrence would satisfy <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></math></span> and hence <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> at every step,\ncontrary to the finite test. The excluded strip is open: equality at its\nboundary already suffices. This proposition proves soundness for a\nspecified <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></math></span>; an irrationality\nargument still needs the quantified family of tests.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:e1","The block norm condition implies\nirrationality","<p><em> Suppose that for every integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and every threshold <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> there are <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> with </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>X</mi><mo>≥</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> and </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>‖</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>X</mi></mrow></munder><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>21</mn><mn>25</mn></mfrac><mi>X</mi><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>. Indeed, the norm bound\nimplies the real-part bound <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo>∑</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>X</mi></math></span>.\nUnder the displayed size condition, this latter bound already gives a\nfinite residue certificate, by <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></math></span> and averaging. The\nargument uses this specific numerical gap; an unspecified saving below\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>X</mi></math></span> is not the stated\nhypothesis.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:e2","A four-tail residue criterion","<p><em>\nFor nonnegative integers <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></math></span>,\ndefine the four-tail combination, its finite numerator and its error\nbound by </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" columnalign=\"right left\" columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"3pt\" data-break-align=\"bottom top\" minlabelspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nConsequently the finite condition </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em>\nimplies <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>. If,\nfor every <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, there\nare <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> satisfying this condition with\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>. Rationality would\ninstead make both diagonal differences in <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> integral for every sufficiently\nlarge <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em>The error bound groups the remainder as </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nEach parenthesis is nonnegative and at most <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, by <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. Their difference\ntherefore has absolute value at most <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. This proves the bound without\nasserting optimality for the actual totient tails. Primality of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi></math></span> is not required by the criterion; a\nprime <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi></math></span> is useful when comparing\nconsecutive LCM heights. The checked instance is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, for which <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>18834</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>282</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:NI-01","An equivalent diagonal\nnonintegrality condition","<p><em> The following statements are equivalent:\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> This is an exact reformulation of irrationality, not a\nweaker theorem about a finite set of scales. The later sufficient\nconditions seek a finite inequality that establishes the right-hand\nside.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:AR-07","A sufficient short-window\ncondition","<p><em> Suppose that for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> there are <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>. Writing out the\ncertificate, the required inequality is </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> It places the residue away from both\nendpoints of the modulus interval. The examples at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></math></span> establish the bounded statement in\nProposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:SK-02\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:SK-02\">6.67</a>, not\nthe condition for every threshold. Pointwise completeness allows a\nsufficiently large depth but does not supply a depth below <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:SEP-03","A sufficient approximation\ncondition","<p><em> Use the prescribed index <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></math></span> and the error <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></msub></math></span> defined above.\nSuppose </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>32</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>. The estimate\n<span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></msub></math></span>\nimplies <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>32</mn></math></span>\nfor every integer <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>z</mi></math></span>, and hence the\nfinite residue separation from which the proof derives irrationality.\nThis is a fixed separation condition along an unbounded family of LCM\nscales. It is not an irrationality-measure theorem for arbitrary\nrational approximants, and nonintegrality alone does not give the stated\nmargin. The depth is prescribed, not an additional free\nwitness.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:TE-04","A sufficient upper-endpoint\nseparation","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></math></span>, and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. Assume </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Define the\nfinite condition </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" columnalign=\"right left\" columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"3pt\" data-break-align=\"bottom top\" minlabelspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd><msub><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">:⟺</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> implies <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>. In\nparticular, irrationality follows if for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span> there are <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></math></span> satisfying <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em>The sign theorem forces an integral tail to give a residue in the\nupper endpoint interval. This one-sided test excludes that interval; it\ndoes not require a lower residue bound. Both the sign-range hypothesis\nand the strict modulus bound are essential premises of this implication.\nThe required unbounded family is not established.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:TE-05","Relations among the sufficient\nconditions","<p><em> The sufficient conditions do not form a single linear\nchain. The proved implications are </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" columnalign=\"right left\" columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"3pt\" data-break-align=\"bottom top\" minlabelspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd><mtext>odd-prefix interval at the prescribed depth</mtext></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mtext> </mtext><mtext>centred-residue bound at the prescribed depth</mtext><mo>,</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext>centred-residue bound at the prescribed depth</mtext></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mtext> </mtext><mtext>centred-residue bound at a variable depth</mtext><mo>,</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext>centred-residue bound at a variable depth</mtext></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mtext> </mtext><mtext>adjacent-suffix band</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mtext> </mtext><mtext>upper-endpoint test</mtext><mo>.</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p><em> There is also a separate sufficient argument:\nterminal dominance and flexible centred magnitude each imply the\ntwo-sided exclusion in Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:TE-06\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:TE-06\">6.66</a>, which implies diagonal\nnonintegrality. These statements refer to the quantified versions, with\nthe bounds on <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></math></span> and the depth\nretained. The detailed conditions and sources appear in\nProposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:te-chain\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:te-chain\">9.14</a>. No\nreverse implication between terminal dominance and flexible magnitude is\nasserted, and none of the required unbounded families is proved\nhere.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:TE-06","An exact endpoint formula","<p><em>\nLet <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></math></span>, and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. Assume </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> If <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></math></span>, then\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nConsequently either of the inequalities </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" columnalign=\"right left\" columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"3pt\" data-break-align=\"bottom top\" minlabelspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> is sufficient for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>. Under\nintegrality the recurrence equals a positive tail difference strictly\nsmaller than <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span>, and the\nidentity rules out both displayed inequalities. The identity is exact;\nthe resulting nonintegrality criterion is only sufficient. Its converse\nis not asserted.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:SK-02","A sufficient extension of the\nexamples through exponent 6","<p><em> The supplied finite result is </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The witnesses at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>23</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>93</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> give this bounded family. A\nwitness with <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></math></span> would extend the\nverified range, but any finite extension would still leave the assertion\nfor arbitrarily large thresholds in Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:AR-07\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:AR-07\">6.62</a> unproved.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:FR-01","A sufficient extremal-order\ncondition","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>. If </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">min</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em>\nthen </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nIf instead the middle value is strictly larger than both outer values,\nthe displayed second difference is negative. In each case the claim\nfollows by adding the two strict differences. No lower bound on their\nsizes is needed.</em></p>\n<p><em>For <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub></math></span>, this reduces\nnonvanishing of one second difference to an ordering of three totient\nvalues. For a prescribed <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi></math></span>, the\nremaining question is whether either strict ordering occurs for\narbitrarily large <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi></math></span>. The elementary\nimplication does not establish those occurrences.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:CP-06","A directed certificate condition","<p><em> For fixed <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, a\ndepth <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> satisfying\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mo>≤</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mtext> </mtext><mo>≤</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> exists if and only if <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>. The endpoint\ninequalities are non-strict.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:CP-07","A sufficient condition at the\nprescribed mod-four pulses","<p><em> Suppose that for every positive\ninteger <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi></math></span> and every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> there are a prime <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>B</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> such that </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≡</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em>\nand every integer <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>z</mi></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>z</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.444em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> has an index <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi></math></span> satisfying </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>. Under a hypothetical\neventual period <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi></math></span>, the proved\ncongruence argument at a sufficiently large such prime forces <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></math></span> to be an integer in the\ntested residue class. The finite test excludes every candidate in that\nclass, a contradiction.</em></p>\n<p><em>The mod-four congruence is supplied by Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:CP-05-inv\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:CP-05-inv\">6.91</a>; exclusion at the\nsame prime is not. Restricting to <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.444em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> retains one residue class out of\nfour, not necessarily exactly one quarter of the candidates in a finite\ninterval. The quantifier over every bound <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi></math></span> cannot be replaced by one fixed prime\nfor each <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:A10","The full quantified condition","<p><em> </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>h</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>N</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> This is exactly the quantifier structure\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>L</mi><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. The quantified condition is\nnot supplied here. The equivalence specifies exactly what a\nresidue-based proof would need beyond the finite examples.</em></p>"]],"source":"erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.json?v=4457e47d5986"}
